Anticoagulant-Induced Major Bleeding

1. Patient Details

Patient Initials / Identifier: C.R.

Age: 67 years

Gender: Female

Weight: 85 kg

2. Suspected Adverse Reaction

Description of Reaction: Spontaneous epistaxis (nosebleeds) lasting >2 hours, extensive hematomas on limbs, and INR elevated to 6.8.

Date of Reaction Onset: Week 3 after introducing a new interacting drug.

Severity: Severe (high risk of major hemorrhage).

3. Suspected Medication(s)

Primary Suspect: Warfarin 5 mg daily (taken for atrial fibrillation).

Interacting Agent: Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Co-trimoxazole) 800/160 mg twice daily added 5 days prior for a UTI.

Action Taken: Warfarin held; Co-trimoxazole discontinued.

4. Concomitant Medications

Medication: Metoprolol 50 mg daily.

5. Outcome & Action Taken

Action Taken: Warfarin held; oral Vitamin K administered to reduce INR.

De-challenge Outcome: INR normalized to 2.3 within 48 hours; bleeding ceased.

Re-challenge: Warfarin restarted at a modified dose post-infection without the interacting antibiotic.

Patient Outcome: Recovered safely without transfusion requirements.
